{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>As far as the place of Shechem.</b> For in that period there was no built city there, only the place." "<b>As far as Elon Moreh.</b> Rashi explained: He showed him Mount Gerizim and Mount Ebal, as is said in the portion of Re'eh, by Elon Moreh. And Elon Moreh and Elonei Mamrei are one." "<b>And the Canaanite was then in the land.</b> Rashi explained: he was going about conquering the Land of Israel from the seed of Shem, because Ham his father was two years older than Shem his uncle. Rashi further explained: the land itself fell to the portion of Shem when Noah divided it. One may object: in the portion of Shelach and in the portion of Ekev, Rashi explained that Ham built Hebron for his son Canaan — if so, it implies that the land of Canaan fell to the portion of Ham. Rather, one may answer: regarding Shem, which Rashi explained here — that he was conquering the Land of Israel from the seed of Shem — perhaps this was in part of the land and not all of it: in the northern eastern lowland, which fell to the portion of Shem, and in that portion was Jerusalem. And the land of the Canaanites was spreading toward the land of Egypt, their brothers, to the south of the world, and in it was Hebron. But according to the language of the Talmud in tractate Ketubot, it works out well: 'Is it possible that a man builds a house for his younger son and afterward for his older son?' etc. — for there it does not deal with Ham at all, but with Noah. And thus one may explain: Noah built Hebron for Shem his younger son, and then built Zoan for Ham his older son. This is difficult: rather, certainly he built Zoan for Ham his son first, and then built Hebron for Shem his son, younger than him, for Ham was two years older than Shem, as is stated in the chapter of Ben Sorer uMoreh. And the fact that it counts Shem first — by way of importance it counts. And now it works out well, that Hebron fell to the portion of Shem." "<b>And the Canaanite was then in the land.</b> He was going about conquering the Land of Israel from the seed of Shem, by force. <b>And the Canaanite was then in the land.</b> It was written with reference to the future, when Israel would be holding the land — therefore the word <b>אז</b> applies to it, meaning: then, in the days of Abraham, the Canaanite was in the land. And similarly further on: <b>והכנעני והפרזי אז יושב בארץ</b>."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:04:04.527499697Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}